{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 28,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.597,
            0.071,
            0.813,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": "Two Variables, One Line"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.841,
            0.071,
            0.881,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": "113"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.11,
            0.852,
            0.13
          ],
          "content": "Consider the following pairs of linear equations in two variables."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.134,
            0.471,
            0.153
          ],
          "content": "Pair 1: 2x + y = 6 and x – y = 2"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.157,
            0.485,
            0.176
          ],
          "content": "Pair 2: x + y = 4 and 2x + 2y = 8"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.18,
            0.519,
            0.2
          ],
          "content": "Pair 3: 3x – 2y = 6 and 6x – 4y = 12"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.204,
            0.495,
            0.224
          ],
          "content": "Pair 4: x – 2y = 4 and 2x – 4y = 6"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.229,
            0.881,
            0.27
          ],
          "content": "For each pair, prepare a table of values (with at least two ordered pairs). Plot the points on the Cartesian plane. Draw the straight lines."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.136,
            0.283,
            0.4,
            0.307
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think and Reflect"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.32,
            0.854,
            0.379
          ],
          "content": "How do we find solutions from the graph? What can we say about the number of solutions when the two lines (i) intersect at a point, (ii) are parallel, (iii) are coincident?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.392,
            0.879,
            0.43
          ],
          "content": "Solutions to the pair of equations are points that lie on both lines. Thus"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.195,
            0.43,
            0.881,
            0.468
          ],
          "content": "(i) when the lines intersect in a single point, there is a unique solution;"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.188,
            0.469,
            0.83,
            0.488
          ],
          "content": "(ii) when the lines are parallel, there are no solutions; and"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.183,
            0.489,
            0.881,
            0.526
          ],
          "content": "(iii) when the lines are coincident, there are infinitely many solutions."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.532,
            0.881,
            0.572
          ],
          "content": "**Example 11:** Solve the pair of linear equations $x + 3y = 6$ and 2x – 3y = 12 graphically. Comment on the nature of the solution."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.576,
            0.881,
            0.631
          ],
          "content": "Consider the equation x + 3y = 6. Expressing y in terms of x, we get $y = { \\frac { 6 - x } { 3 } }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.63,
            0.882,
            0.689
          ],
          "content": "Considering x to be first 0 and then 6 we obtain the corresponding values of y as 2 and 0 respectively. Hence (0, 2) and (6, 0) are points on the line."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.367,
            0.689,
            0.631,
            0.754
          ],
          "content": "| x | 0 | 6 |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| y | 2 | 0 |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_28_table_17.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.761,
            0.883,
            0.841
          ],
          "content": "Now consider the equation 2x – 3y = 12. Expressing y in terms of $X ,$ we $\\mathrm{get}y = \\frac{2x - 12}{3}$ . Considering x to be 0 and then 3, we obtain the values of y as –4, and –2 respectively. Hence (0, –4) and (3, –2) are points on the line."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.369,
            0.846,
            0.631,
            0.911
          ],
          "content": "| x | 0 | 3 |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| y | - 4 | - 2 |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_28_table_19.jpg"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
